# wireguard-go: the userspace WireGuard daemon, in Go. Fully static without
# asking, because CGO is off on this backend.
#
# This is the half that moves packets. It creates the tun interface and then
# waits: every key, peer and endpoint is configured over a UNIX socket at
# /var/run/wireguard/<iface>.sock, and nothing in this binary can write to that
# socket. The `wg` tool does, and it is the packages/wireguard-tools half --
# read out of main.go, which has no configuration parser at all.
#
# Two packages rather than one because the backend is per package: the go
# backend blanks CC (lib/toolchain.sh), so a Go daemon and a C tool cannot come
# out of the same recipe. They are separate upstreams with separate versions
# anyway.
PKG=wireguard-go
VERSION=0.0.20250522
REVISION=1
SOURCE=https://github.com/WireGuard/wireguard-go/archive/refs/tags/0.0.20250522.tar.gz
SHA256=70d0448d289a691978920f615c687864c3d7b0379d6f974e4f3590487dff35f7
TOOLCHAIN=go
# No armv7-neon, for the reason packages/speedtest gives: Go's GOARM=7 has no
# NEON variant, so that build would be byte-identical to armv7, and it could
# not satisfy the NEON gate either because Go emits no ARM build attributes.
# One armv7 build serves both kinds of box; install.sh degrades to it.
TARGETS='x86_64 i686 armv5 armv7 aarch64 mips mipsel'

SMOKE='--version'
SMOKE_EXPECT='wireguard-go v0.0.20250522'

# UNVERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE, twice over:
#
#  * /dev/net/tun. Without the tun driver in the box's kernel this daemon
#    cannot create an interface and there is nothing the binary can do about
#    it. Most Enigma2 and OpenWrt kernels have it; yours is the one that counts.
#  * Go's kernel floor. The same caveat packages/speedtest carries: Go's
#    documented minimum is newer than the 2.6.32 a VU+ Solo2 runs, and qemu
#    cannot answer the question because it services syscalls from the host
#    kernel. If it fails there, the way out is a C WireGuard -- the kernel
#    module where the box has one, or boringtun.
